WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.293 --> 00:00:04.126
- Do what I love.

00:00:04.126 --> 00:00:06.642
- Choose something that you love to do.

00:00:06.642 --> 00:00:07.881
- I really love what I'm doing.

00:00:07.881 --> 00:00:09.477
- This is what I want to do.

00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:10.377
- Hello Believe Nation.

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:14.011
Today we're going to talk
about how to love what you do.

00:00:14.011 --> 00:00:16.140
And as always, guys, as
you're watching the videos,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.377
if you see something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:18.377 --> 00:00:20.065
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:20.065 --> 00:00:22.692
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired.

00:00:22.692 --> 00:00:24.526
And if you write it down,
it's much more likely

00:00:24.526 --> 00:00:27.109
to stick with yourself as well.

00:00:28.224 --> 00:00:31.391
(inspirational music)

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.019
- I'm a very spiritual person,
and I follow my spirit.

00:00:40.019 --> 00:00:44.609
And I do what my spirit
tells me to do, you know.

00:00:44.609 --> 00:00:46.517
And I don't have a set,

00:00:46.517 --> 00:00:48.335
like, "Oh, I want to play
this type of character

00:00:48.335 --> 00:00:50.113
"and I won't play that type of character."

00:00:50.113 --> 00:00:53.613
I'm receptive to anything, you know.

00:00:53.613 --> 00:00:55.715
And when the right thing comes along,

00:00:55.715 --> 00:00:58.904
if it's serious, if it's
funny, if it's silly,

00:00:58.904 --> 00:01:01.141
if it's low budget.

00:01:01.141 --> 00:01:05.197
Put it this way, I just
do whatever makes me,

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:09.114
I get the most gratification
from as an artist.

00:01:10.092 --> 00:01:12.510
I do music albums and
nobody buys my records.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.154
I do them because I love
making music, you know.

00:01:15.154 --> 00:01:17.044
And that's the same way I am about movies.

00:01:17.044 --> 00:01:19.047
I don't make movies because,

00:01:19.047 --> 00:01:21.016
I don't go into it
thinking, "Let me do this

00:01:21.016 --> 00:01:22.851
"because then people will come."

00:01:22.851 --> 00:01:24.065
"How much will the movie make?"

00:01:24.065 --> 00:01:25.953
"I'm trying to make the audience do that."

00:01:25.953 --> 00:01:28.901
I do what feels right for me.

00:01:28.901 --> 00:01:31.568
- What were you like as a child?

00:01:31.568 --> 00:01:35.485
- I was a combination of
reclusive and obsessed

00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:37.952
with what I wanted to do.

00:01:37.952 --> 00:01:41.053
When I was two, I was
sitting in front of the TV

00:01:41.053 --> 00:01:43.769
drawing Fred Flintstones
and Woody Woodpeckers.

00:01:43.769 --> 00:01:45.413
And at age seven I'm running around

00:01:45.413 --> 00:01:47.517
like a crazy person doing voices.

00:01:47.517 --> 00:01:50.476
But at the same time, I would
try to make animated films,

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:54.696
and try to figure out exactly
how that art form worked.

00:01:54.696 --> 00:01:56.112
- When did you do your first cartoon?

00:01:56.112 --> 00:01:57.586
How old were you and what was it?

00:01:57.586 --> 00:01:59.398
- I was in seventh grade.

00:01:59.398 --> 00:02:01.659
It was called Space
Pirates, it was terrible.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:03.695
- I don't know, I don't give
a (word blanked out) about

00:02:03.695 --> 00:02:07.445
trying to make what I
think people will like.

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:14.125
I think because it doesn't
sound like one full album

00:02:14.125 --> 00:02:17.191
from top to bottom, people disregard it.

00:02:17.191 --> 00:02:20.035
Because it's all over the
place, people think it's messy

00:02:20.035 --> 00:02:23.305
or chaotic, but I think
it's beautiful chaos

00:02:23.305 --> 00:02:24.722
and it's perfect.

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:28.347
It's my favorite album to date,

00:02:28.347 --> 00:02:30.930
regardless of what anyone says.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.139
I could understand why
people don't like it.

00:02:34.139 --> 00:02:36.974
It's different, and sometimes different

00:02:36.974 --> 00:02:38.763
doesn't equal good to some people.

00:02:38.763 --> 00:02:42.263
But I love it and, you know, that's great.

00:02:44.871 --> 00:02:46.469
That's all that matters in the end.

00:02:46.469 --> 00:02:50.452
If you make something you
a hundred percent love,

00:02:50.452 --> 00:02:53.805
and everyone loves it, that's
a (word blanked out) win-win.

00:02:53.805 --> 00:02:54.638
That's great.

00:02:54.638 --> 00:02:56.957
If you have something that you really love

00:02:56.957 --> 00:02:58.749
and everyone (word blanked out) hates it,

00:02:58.749 --> 00:03:00.702
at least you (word blanked out) love it,

00:03:00.702 --> 00:03:02.307
and you just move on.

00:03:02.307 --> 00:03:05.269
- I woke up every morning excited.

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:06.778
I was completely jacked.

00:03:06.778 --> 00:03:08.846
I've never been more excited
to go to work every day

00:03:08.846 --> 00:03:10.995
as I was on this project.

00:03:10.995 --> 00:03:14.112
At the end of the day, I'd
never been more drained

00:03:14.112 --> 00:03:15.260
in my life.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.083
But somehow, some way, I
found the energy every morning

00:03:18.083 --> 00:03:21.128
to get up and just go for it.

00:03:21.128 --> 00:03:23.753
The further we got into it,
the more Wayne was just like,

00:03:23.753 --> 00:03:27.858
"Okay, do what you want to
do, do what you want to do."

00:03:27.858 --> 00:03:29.995
I never had that kind of freedom before.

00:03:29.995 --> 00:03:32.662
- You know, I have tons
of things I'd like to do,

00:03:32.662 --> 00:03:35.351
and life is short, unfortunately.

00:03:35.351 --> 00:03:37.094
But I hope it will be long enough for me

00:03:37.094 --> 00:03:38.761
to do some of these.

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:44.240
I have known a few
writers who have retired.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:47.332
I cannot imagine ever retiring.

00:03:47.332 --> 00:03:50.155
Writing and being a writer is too much

00:03:50.155 --> 00:03:55.004
part of my identity, and
too much part of what I am.

00:03:55.004 --> 00:03:56.962
I don't know what I would
do with all that time

00:03:56.962 --> 00:03:58.559
if I did stop writing, you know.

00:03:58.559 --> 00:04:00.472
Play video games or something like that?

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:02.666
Watch a lot more television?

00:04:02.666 --> 00:04:04.927
It seem unlikely to me.

00:04:04.927 --> 00:04:07.740
I've always told stories, whether I could

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:08.932
make my living from it.

00:04:08.932 --> 00:04:11.936
Or even when I was a little
kid just making up stories

00:04:11.936 --> 00:04:13.501
for the other kids in the neighborhood.

00:04:13.501 --> 00:04:17.820
And I think I'm going to continue
to do that until the end.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:21.546
- When I talk to pre
first time film makers

00:04:21.546 --> 00:04:24.908
or first time film makers,
I always tell them,

00:04:24.908 --> 00:04:26.908
"Look, making movies is

00:04:28.025 --> 00:04:29.993
eating a sandwich of (word blanked out).

00:04:29.993 --> 00:04:32.064
And the only thing that
gets a little better is,

00:04:32.064 --> 00:04:35.045
as the years go by, you
get a little more bread.

00:04:35.045 --> 00:04:37.621
But the (word blanked out) always there.

00:04:37.621 --> 00:04:39.950
- [Interviewer] Isn't
that true of life also?

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:40.839
- I think it is.

00:04:40.839 --> 00:04:44.822
I mean, I use another
metaphor for life (laughs).

00:04:44.822 --> 00:04:48.028
But for film, I tell you, that one fits.

00:04:48.028 --> 00:04:51.842
- [Interviewer] But in the
end, somehow, you find the joy?

00:04:51.842 --> 00:04:54.430
- Well, you end up convincing yourself

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:59.121
that it's peanut butter (laughs).

00:04:59.121 --> 00:05:02.296
- [Interviewer] And is
it ever peanut butter?

00:05:02.296 --> 00:05:05.401
- I want to believe it is (laughs).

00:05:05.401 --> 00:05:07.831
- [Interviewer] But do
you look back and go,

00:05:07.831 --> 00:05:09.721
"Yes, I did that..."

00:05:09.721 --> 00:05:12.761
- I tell you, without a
doubt we may say this,

00:05:12.761 --> 00:05:15.178
we may say it's the greatest.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:18.845
Being an artist in whatever discipline,

00:05:18.845 --> 00:05:22.209
dance, painting, theater,
whatever you want,

00:05:22.209 --> 00:05:24.665
it's the greatest work on earth.

00:05:24.665 --> 00:05:27.665
I mean, it's truly this
hard thing in this way

00:05:27.665 --> 00:05:30.039
or that way, but so is every job.

00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:31.856
So is every job.

00:05:31.856 --> 00:05:35.648
When I was younger, I
worked with my father

00:05:35.648 --> 00:05:38.157
on real estate for about five years.

00:05:38.157 --> 00:05:40.539
I was the worst car salesman

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:44.049
in the history of car salesmanship.

00:05:44.049 --> 00:05:45.017
- [Interviewer] Because
you were too honest?

00:05:45.017 --> 00:05:46.648
- Yeah, people would come
and say, "What about this?"

00:05:46.648 --> 00:05:47.931
I would say, "no."

00:05:47.931 --> 00:05:49.269
(laughs)

00:05:49.269 --> 00:05:53.241
I was Glengarry Glen Ross, I
would not get the knife set.

00:05:53.241 --> 00:05:55.408
I would be Shelley Levene.

00:05:56.459 --> 00:05:58.382
I would be out, out.

00:05:58.382 --> 00:05:59.271
It was terrible.

00:05:59.271 --> 00:06:02.104
My father said, "You're terrible."

00:06:03.383 --> 00:06:06.707
And the other vendors, whenever I went out

00:06:06.707 --> 00:06:09.305
the other salesmen, whenever
I went out to a client,

00:06:09.305 --> 00:06:11.524
they actually laughed behind my back.

00:06:11.524 --> 00:06:15.123
Actually, physically were
laughing behind my back.

00:06:15.123 --> 00:06:16.394
And I was the worst.

00:06:16.394 --> 00:06:17.947
But in real estate I was good.

00:06:17.947 --> 00:06:19.510
I was good in real estate.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:21.693
My father used to say,
"You have to do this

00:06:21.693 --> 00:06:25.375
"to show that you can
have an honest living."

00:06:25.375 --> 00:06:27.560
And what I did was work on
real estate in the mornings

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:29.820
and I did monsters in the afternoon.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.634
But I know that when you have a real job,

00:06:32.634 --> 00:06:36.564
I painted highways one
summer in Wichita, Kansas.

00:06:36.564 --> 00:06:39.834
I mean, a real job has equal heartbreak

00:06:39.834 --> 00:06:42.302
and stuff like that, and politics.

00:06:42.302 --> 00:06:44.390
- [Interviewer] Well, I mean,
I think it's one of the things

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:45.932
that people really misunderstand,

00:06:45.932 --> 00:06:48.283
that as glorious as it is
to be able to do the things

00:06:48.283 --> 00:06:50.746
you're passionate about,
and to be an artist,

00:06:50.746 --> 00:06:52.434
it's still a job.

00:06:52.434 --> 00:06:55.362
- Oh, it's still a job,
yeah, it is, it is.

00:06:55.362 --> 00:06:59.080
But God bless anyone that can do this.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:02.207
It's beautiful and you
have to truly be grateful.

00:07:02.207 --> 00:07:04.299
And the only way to remain objective about

00:07:04.299 --> 00:07:08.466
how grateful you need to
be, is present your present

00:07:09.722 --> 00:07:13.536
to your past self when
you're 20 years old.

00:07:13.536 --> 00:07:15.832
And if you go and talk to
yourself at 20 years old

00:07:15.832 --> 00:07:19.848
and said, "Would you like
to be doing this movie

00:07:19.848 --> 00:07:22.042
"with this budget," what do you think

00:07:22.042 --> 00:07:24.236
your response at age 20 would be?

00:07:24.236 --> 00:07:26.812
Abso(word blanked out)lutely, yes.

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:28.457
- I knew I wanted to be
in the skating industry.

00:07:28.457 --> 00:07:30.491
I love skating too much,
I wanted to be in it.

00:07:30.491 --> 00:07:31.587
I didn't know I would make a living

00:07:31.587 --> 00:07:35.902
as a professional, but I was
devoted to skateboarding.

00:07:35.902 --> 00:07:39.902
So, I refinanced my house
and took all the money

00:07:41.237 --> 00:07:42.675
and started a skateboard company,

00:07:42.675 --> 00:07:44.724
which seems like the stupidest
thing in the world to do

00:07:44.724 --> 00:07:46.660
when skating was dying, but it was

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.426
exactly what I wanted to do.

00:07:49.426 --> 00:07:51.687
I wanted to be a company owner
and be behind the scenes.

00:07:51.687 --> 00:07:53.645
I quit my sponsor and started.

00:07:53.645 --> 00:07:57.562
And for about three years,
struggled very hard.

00:07:58.573 --> 00:08:01.442
My partner and I, we had
pooled our money together.

00:08:01.442 --> 00:08:03.455
He was an ex-pro skater as well.

00:08:03.455 --> 00:08:06.205
And we just did Birdhouse and did

00:08:07.809 --> 00:08:09.685
whatever we could to get by.

00:08:09.685 --> 00:08:11.935
All the team was staying on my couch,

00:08:11.935 --> 00:08:13.736
and literally, while I'm changing diapers,

00:08:13.736 --> 00:08:15.692
I'm driving guys to these spots

00:08:15.692 --> 00:08:17.661
that they're going to get arrested at

00:08:17.661 --> 00:08:19.135
because there's no skate parks,

00:08:19.135 --> 00:08:22.302
so they're going to go ride school yards.

00:08:23.196 --> 00:08:26.065
It was such a strange
existence, but I loved it

00:08:26.065 --> 00:08:27.449
because it was skateboarding

00:08:27.449 --> 00:08:29.070
and it was exactly what I wanted to do.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:31.737
And so, we really hunkered down.

00:08:32.714 --> 00:08:36.798
I was living on Taco Bell
and peanut butter and jelly

00:08:36.798 --> 00:08:37.631
and Top Ramen.

00:08:37.631 --> 00:08:38.464
- Mmm.

00:08:39.487 --> 00:08:41.804
- Probably three years, for sure.

00:08:41.804 --> 00:08:44.246
But I didn't care because I
still got to do what I loved.

00:08:44.246 --> 00:08:45.338
That's the thing is that people think,

00:08:45.338 --> 00:08:46.182
"Was it a struggle?"

00:08:46.182 --> 00:08:50.018
And it's like, well no,
because I loved it too much.

00:08:50.018 --> 00:08:51.729
I still got to go skate.

00:08:51.729 --> 00:08:52.685
And I had time to skate.

00:08:52.685 --> 00:08:55.723
I didn't have to go sit behind
a computer for eight hours

00:08:55.723 --> 00:08:57.545
and then hope I get time to skate.

00:08:57.545 --> 00:09:01.712
- Mark Twain once said,
"The two most important days

00:09:02.608 --> 00:09:06.281
"of your life are the day you were born

00:09:06.281 --> 00:09:08.864
"and the day you find out why."

00:09:09.819 --> 00:09:11.869
The first of those has
already happened to you

00:09:11.869 --> 00:09:15.310
'cause you're sitting right here, kay.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:19.090
The second, the day that you
find out why you were born

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:22.949
may have already happened to many of you.

00:09:22.949 --> 00:09:25.289
And to the others, not yet.

00:09:25.289 --> 00:09:28.214
But you will know when it happens.

00:09:28.214 --> 00:09:32.563
It will be connected to
something that you truly love.

00:09:32.563 --> 00:09:35.528
I've always said to my five sons,

00:09:35.528 --> 00:09:38.295
"Choose something that you love to do

00:09:38.295 --> 00:09:40.837
"and you'll never work
a day in your life."

00:09:40.837 --> 00:09:42.525
- I really love what I'm doing.

00:09:42.525 --> 00:09:43.999
Not just so much where people like,

00:09:43.999 --> 00:09:46.103
"Oh find your passion and go do it."

00:09:46.103 --> 00:09:49.680
A lot of people claim it's
something they really like,

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.324
but I enjoy marketing so much,

00:09:52.324 --> 00:09:54.810
and creating companies for marketers,

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.562
that I work my butt off and
it doesn't seem like work.

00:09:57.562 --> 00:10:00.435
I can work 70, 80 hour weeks consistently

00:10:00.435 --> 00:10:01.785
and not have to worry about it.

00:10:01.785 --> 00:10:03.743
It never stresses me
out, I never feel like,

00:10:03.743 --> 00:10:05.633
"Oh, I should take a
vacation," or any of that.

00:10:05.633 --> 00:10:07.084
I really do enjoy working.

00:10:07.084 --> 00:10:08.344
I'm a workaholic.

00:10:08.344 --> 00:10:10.370
- This is my life.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:12.867
But this is also me livin' it.

00:10:12.867 --> 00:10:16.539
You tune in, and what you
should walk away from,

00:10:16.539 --> 00:10:19.667
what I would want you
most to walk away with

00:10:19.667 --> 00:10:22.584
is the idea of, "Yo, what can I do?

00:10:24.111 --> 00:10:25.821
"What am I doing with my life?

00:10:25.821 --> 00:10:29.388
"Am I living it or am I just
going to some humdrum job

00:10:29.388 --> 00:10:32.369
"that I don't really want to be at,

00:10:32.369 --> 00:10:34.619
"doing some minuscule task.

00:10:35.632 --> 00:10:38.726
"Getting paid to be a
mindless drone, you know.

00:10:38.726 --> 00:10:42.348
"Or am I out there
living life, on my terms,

00:10:42.348 --> 00:10:43.834
"the way I want to live it,

00:10:43.834 --> 00:10:45.822
"doing the things that I want to do?"

00:10:45.822 --> 00:10:48.356
Make no mistake about
it, as hard as this is,

00:10:48.356 --> 00:10:52.023
as hard as this is, this
is what I want to do.

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:55.703
This is what I want to do, you know.

00:10:56.749 --> 00:11:00.507
And some people can make fun
of it, they can crack jokes,

00:11:00.507 --> 00:11:04.174
they can sit back and
analyze and criticize,

00:11:05.626 --> 00:11:10.441
and make all the fun they
want, but I'm living my life.

00:11:10.441 --> 00:11:12.343
I'm doin' it.

00:11:12.343 --> 00:11:13.591
What are you?

00:11:13.591 --> 00:11:14.784
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:11:14.784 --> 00:11:15.785
I hope you enjoyed.

00:11:15.785 --> 00:11:17.068
I'd love to know which of the clips

00:11:17.068 --> 00:11:19.430
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:21.827
Which message resonated with you the most?

00:11:21.827 --> 00:11:23.289
Leave it down in the comments below

00:11:23.289 --> 00:11:25.011
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:11:25.011 --> 00:11:27.092
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Steph Reveron.

00:11:27.092 --> 00:11:29.354
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:11:29.354 --> 00:11:32.132
Your One Word, and taking
that picture and posting it.

00:11:32.132 --> 00:11:34.461
I really, really, really
appreciate your support.

00:11:34.461 --> 00:11:35.856
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:11:35.856 --> 00:11:37.060
I believe in you.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:39.085
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:11:39.085 --> 00:11:42.303
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:11:42.303 --> 00:11:43.803
I'll see you soon.

